NAME
    search - iterative memory value search

SYNOPSIS
    search <space> <value> [<width>]
    search refine <value>
    search changed|unchanged|increased|decreased
    search list [<count>]
    search reset

DESCRIPTION
    Finds unknown memory addresses (such as scores, lives, or flags) by
    scanning a memory space for an initial value and narrowing candidates
    as gameplay progresses.

    One search session is active per shell; starting a new
    'search <space> <value>' replaces any existing candidates. Values and
    counts are parsed as hexadecimal. Refuses spaces backed by live
    hardware registers with read side effects (such as CpuBus); use a
    plain memory space such as WRAM.

OPTIONS
    <space> <value> [<width>]
        Start a new search in <space> for little-endian <value> of byte
        width <width> (1, 2, or 4; default 1).

    refine <value>
        Keep only candidate addresses currently equal to <value>.

    changed | unchanged | increased | decreased
        Keep only candidates whose current value changed, stayed equal,
        increased, or decreased relative to the previous scan or refine.

    list [<count>]
        Print up to <count> current candidates and their live values
        (default 20, parsed as hex).

    reset
        Discard the active search session.

EXAMPLES
    search WRAM 64
    search refine 65
    search decreased
    search list 10
    search reset

EXIT STATUS
    0   Search step completed (or usage/refusal message printed).
    1   No target attached, unknown space, invalid argument, or session
        service fault.

SEE ALSO
    memfind(1), snapshot(1), diff(1), cheat(1), mem(1), spaces(1)
